# Copy to .env and adjust as needed.

# Postgres connection string. The value below matches docker-compose.dev.yml
# (run `npm run db:up` to start that container). Railway injects its own
# DATABASE_URL in production — nothing here is read there.
DATABASE_URL=postgres://mocoweb:mocoweb@localhost:5433/mocoweb

# S3-compatible object storage for media (src/lib/server/cms/media/). Local
# dev talks to the SeaweedFS container in docker-compose.dev.yml (also
# started by `npm run db:up`); the credentials below match
# docker/seaweedfs-s3-config.json. In production, Railway Buckets injects
# these five variables itself — nothing here is read there, and the client
# code has no provider-specific logic, only these env vars.
BUCKET=mocoweb-media
ENDPOINT=http://localhost:9000
ACCESS_KEY_ID=devaccesskeyid
SECRET_ACCESS_KEY=devsecretaccesskey
REGION=us-east-1

# CMS OAuth engine (src/lib/server/cms/auth/). This is the ONLY credential
# in the whole system — there are no user accounts, no passwords. It is the
# key an MCP client (Claude Desktop, Codex, ...) is given to connect. Every
# other key the auth engine needs (JWT signing, refresh-token hashing) is
# derived from this one with HKDF, which is what makes rotating it a kill
# switch: change it and every previously issued access/refresh token stops
# working immediately, no revocation table, no redeploy.
#
# NEVER hand-write this value — a person-chosen key becomes "moco2026".
# Generate one with:
#   node scripts/generate-owner-key.ts
OWNER_KEY=

# Optional, test-only override for how long an authorization code lives
# (seconds; default 120). Nothing outside .migration/auth-verify.ts should
# ever need to set this.
# OAUTH_CODE_TTL_SECONDS=120

# adapter-node builds every self-referencing absolute URL (used throughout
# the OAuth engine: the issuer, /authorize, /token, /register, and the
# resource_metadata link in a 401's WWW-Authenticate) from the incoming
# request -- and assumes https unless told otherwise. Railway terminates TLS
# at the edge and proxies to this app over plain HTTP, so without this set,
# those URLs would still say https (which happens to be correct for
# Railway's own domains) but ORIGIN is the documented, explicit way to pin
# it rather than rely on that default. Required for local testing against
# `node build` over plain HTTP (see .migration/auth-verify.ts), where the
# https default is simply wrong:
# ORIGIN=https://your-app.up.railway.app

# Contact form notification email (Lane B4, src/lib/server/cms/notify/email.ts).
# Provider: Resend (resend.com) — free tier, no card required. Sign up with
# the SAME address contact-form notifications should land in (NOTIFY_TO_EMAIL
# below): Resend's zero-setup sandbox sender (onboarding@resend.dev) only
# delivers to the Resend account's own address, which is exactly this site's
# use case (notifying the studio's own inbox) — no DNS records needed. If you
# ever want a branded "from" address, or to notify a DIFFERENT address than
# the Resend account's own, you must verify a domain you own in Resend
# (adds DNS TXT/CNAME records) — that is a real setup step a non-technical
# designer likely cannot do alone; see the Lane B4 report.
#
# Get a key at https://resend.com/api-keys after signing up.
RESEND_API_KEY=

# Where the "new inquiry" notification email is sent. Must match the email
# address the Resend account was created with unless a custom domain is
# verified (see above). Defaults to mocoestudiocreativo@gmail.com if unset.
# NOTIFY_TO_EMAIL=mocoestudiocreativo@gmail.com

# The "From" address on the notification email. Defaults to Resend's
# zero-setup sandbox sender if unset — only change this once a custom domain
# is verified in Resend.
# NOTIFY_FROM_EMAIL=Moco Web <onboarding@resend.dev>
